The growth of online sales slowed as much as possible in August, and the reason for this may be Black Friday
In the first three months of 2015, an increase in online sales by seven per cent
Last month was the slowest storing since it began to be observed in the year of the year 2000
Online Snakes Became Involved Three Years Ago Withdrawal of Mobile Devices
Experts are convinced that the November Black Friday could contribute to the deterrence of spending
For about a period of about a decade, virtual shopping has been experiencing a boom, transforming many central streets in Britain into ghost cities.
But experts say that now there are signs of equalization of online sales, after the latest data showed only a seven percent increase during the first three months of 2015.
The total cost of online shopping in August showed a minimal increase since they began to be recorded in the year 2000, an increase of only five per cent.
Experts say that now there are signs of equalization of online sales, after the latest data showed only a seven percent increase during the first three months of 2015
“If you take a look at the growth rate in the previous years and go back to the year of 2000, they’ve always found a sensitious explanation,” Andy Malkehi, ISMG’s Sales Form Index, told the Guardian.
He explained that online sales activated three years ago, when buyers began to use their mobile phones to make purchases.
This meant that even more people had the opportunity to buy online, and that they could have done it under any conditions, for example, sitting on the couch.
But now it goes out on the plateau, and you can see a decrease (growth).
The phenomenon of Black Floor, which came from America, is mentioned as one of the reasons for the slowdown in sales.
Experts believe that the buyers could refrain from shopping on the eve, which takes place on the last Friday of November.
Last year, in an amount of five times more than the average, rushed to the computers in order to have time to take advantage of favorable offers, thereby bringing down a number of seller websites.
Experts believe that the Black Friday, when the buyers enter a real battle for profitable purchases, could force people to abstain from shopping
According to a study published by the British Retail Consortium, just over 1 pound sterling out of every 5 is spent online.
As for such goods as televisions and washing machines, more than one-third they are sold through online stores.
At the same time, more than a quarter of people buy clothes and shoe online more willingly than in fashionable shops on central streets.
