U.S. Federal Personnel Interest Rate
The world is overseen by the Federal Reserve Bank's meeting, which discusses within two days an important decision - to raise the interest rate for the first time in the last decade.
A survey of the 80 Economists, conducted by Reuters, found that a little more than half of those who last week believed that the Federal Reserve will go for it, now believe that it will still wait and keep the rate at the current range of 0-0.25 percent.
This is a historical event, as it has been seven years since the Federal Reserve lowered the bet to zero.
For the for the first time, we come to the fact that the main bank can shift from the zero level and begin the process of normalization of interest rates, ”said Angel Ubide, a leading member of the Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The U.S. economy is working effectively, the economic recovery adds trillions of dollars to the balance sheet, and inflation is succulent.
However, the Federal Reserve cannot ignite how the solution will affect the global economy.
He warned the markets to prepare for the changes, but everything speaks about their confidence that all chances are against such a step.
The decision will be announced at 20.00 CET on Thursday.
A new Balkan route: the refugees are going to the EU through Croatia.
“Disclose it!” chanted hundreds of refugees chanted at the Khorgos checkpoint on the Serbo-Hungarian border.
From Tuesday, Budapest has blocked the border for the settlers.
Asygnant seekers are already beginning to probe other options for routes to the European Union.
One of them, bypassing the enclosed border with Serbia, lies through Romania.
The other - through Hortavia and Slovenia - to Austria.
According to some reports, the buses, which were carrying refugees from Serbia to Hungary, are now heading to the city of Shida on the Croatian border.
Drivers received orders from the authorities to follow without stopping.
Srpsian Interior Minister Nebojesha Stepanovich assures that his country is doing everything in its power to help the refugees, but its capabilities are limited:
It’s our job to secure Serbia as far as possible.
On the other hand, we also need to help these people, to ensure a humane attitude towards them.
But that’s a pan-to-European problem.
To solve it is not easy, no country in Europe alone will not cope with this, and even more so such a small country as Serbia.
Belgrade asks Budapest to open a border for refugees who don’t want to stay in Serbia.
Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán relented:
If someone is called a refugee, in Serbia he must fill out the necessary documents for this.
And if he doesn't do it, he won't be allowed into our country, because it's quite safe in Serbia.
Hungary has already erected a 17-kilometer fence on the border with Serbia.
But Orban does not intend to dwell on this: he plans to plunder the same fence on the border with Romania.
Bucharest has already stated that this is incompatible with European principles.
Vangelice Maymarakis: “Europe wants to help us.”
Vangelis Meymaracis for a number of years was a member of the center-right governments, which, according to the left, led the country to a financial crisis.
Now, becoming the interim leader of the New Democrat party, is challenging Tsipras.
A few days before the elections, he met with journalist Euronews.
Euronews: Mr. Meymarakis, welcome to Euronews!
Vangelice Meimarakis: Thank you for inviting.
Ninth months ago, your party suffered a crushing defeat, ceding SYRIZA coalition, but now, judging by polls, “New democracy” has equaled it in popularity.
What's changed since then?
Vangelice Maymaracis: Greece has Changed, and so did we.
Mr. Tsipras lied a lot last January.
Nine months ago, Greece had a budget surplus and prospects for economic growth.
Now the country again has a budget deficit and a high level of unemployment.
Investors left because Mr. Tsipras chased them.
Over the last nine months, the Greeks have had the opportunity to estimate the situation in which the country was in January, and Mr. Tsipraz’s false promises.
And now they can easily compare the promise with what actually happened.
Everyday life became noticeably worse compared with January, when the situation improved, and this applied to the majority of Greek citizens.
Euronews: But you’re saying that if you win an election, you’ll aspire to alliances with other parties, including SYRIZ.
Vangelice Maymarakis: It’s a fact that, as we’ve understood in recent years, Greece can’t move forward without understanding and co-operation.
And it is no coincidence that in all the other countries where rescue programs have operated or are still operating, political forces and all the ruling parties have come together in order to carry out a program according to which they reached an agreement without renouncing their political or ideological autonomy.
That is why I firmly believe that at the present juncture only the cooperation of political forces and institutions will allow the country to move forward.
Euronews: Your party voted in favor of the agreement between Mr. Tsipras and the country’s lenders.
If you’re going to be a new premier on Monday, would you follow this deal, or will you try to change it at least in part?
Vangeliz Meymarakis: In fact, we have voted for the movement of Greece on the European path, because we are well aware of what disastrous the country's withdrawal from the EU would lead to.
Now Europe is assisting us by providing subsidies to our farmers for their products, we have subsoil funds, a Juncker package and assistance in the banking sector.
All this would have been lost, if there had been no treaty with Europe.
But the current accrual accrual measures were the result of how Mr. Tsipras negotiated.
I don't know that Europe doesn't wanna destroy us.
“Europe wants to help us.
Thus, if Europe sees that the new Greek government during the first year in power wants to restore order, acts rationally and makes it clear that the country has a strong political will to implement the salvage program, then, I am sure, all the commitments made that cannot be fulfilled because they lead to a recess, can again become the subject of negotiations, but only after a certain time.
It would be erroneculous to assume that already in the first year of the new agreement, we will be able to return to negotiations with our creditors, since they are expected to fully implement the agreement.
If we want to restore their confidence, we must continue to privatize, reforms and the structural changes that have been negotiated, continuing to enjoy the benefits of EU financing and the great investment opportunities that we need to open for a better understanding and cooperation with Europe.
Regrettably, during the talks, Tsipras government did not present any serious arguments on many key political issues, which we found out when discussing these issues with our European colleagues.
Let me give you an example related to farmers’ income.
We explained to our European partners that we are not talking about a fixed monthly income, and therefore it can’t be fully taxed in advance.
We were told that Tsipras' ministers have never talked about this.
That is, the Greek ministers were unprepared and illustrious, and therefore unsuccessfully conducted negotiations and concluded an unsuccessful agreement.
Euronews: If you win the election, you will soon be at the same table with Mr. Juncker, Hollande, Madger Merkel and other European leaders.
Shall it be a difference what you will tell them from what your predecessor Antonis Samaras said to them?
Vangelice Maymaracis: First of all, I will explain to them that by the end of 2014 we tried best to fulfill the agreement of the year of the year of 2012, bypassing the pointed corners in it.
We pushed the reforms forward.
Of course, we lacked the necessary resolution and speed, but we acted in extraordinary circumstances.
The market has fallen, and there are no potential buyers who were ready to pay the price, which suited the Greek government.
I at that time was the speaker of the parliament, I was able to agree on the opinions of the three parliamentary groups, which then constituted a governmental majority.
And despite their serious ideological disagreements on the key bills, we had a functioning parliament, and this is in the presence of populists and extremists, as well as the mass demonstrations taking place from the outside.
And we were able to lead the country to a normal situation, which would continue to improve, if the process was not interrupted by the early general elections resulting from the December presidential elections.
Euronews: In the last few weeks, a massive flow of refugees has been looming through the territory of Greece.
What do you think about this situation, and about the reaction of the European countries these people aspire to?
Vangelis Meymarakis: The crisis with refugees is a ticking time bomb that we will have to deal with after winning the election.
And the reason is that the SYRIZA government did not act in this situation as it should.
In such matters, you should act ahead of schedule, because if the problem overtakes you, there are only extraordinary measures left.
And now we're in this situation.
“Especially the interim government that replaced SYRIZ has done more in just seven days than Mr. Tsipras did in just seven months.
I am sure that these actions of the interim government, which is provided with valuable assistance by the President of the Republic, who called for an EU summit, will make it possible to find a solution.
We need to see how many refugees will be able to leave the country, and note that refugees are not illegal immigrants.
These are the victims of the war in Syria, the victims of some extraordinary events that have taken place with them.
This issue has a deeply humanitarian aspect, and we will adhere to the position that European politicians must adopt in this new situation.
The corresponding decision should be made at the EU summit, the United Nations should also be involved.
We also need better coordination of action, more effective border controls and reception centres.
We also need to effectively use the economic assistance we receive from the EU in order to be able to cope with this pressing problem.
Euronews: Mr. Maymaracis, thank you for the interview.
Vangelice Maymarakis: Thank you.
