"Sonotogenetics" allows you to control brain cells using sound waves
The video celebrates how nematodes change the direction of motion at the moment when they are exposed to sound pulses.
Scholar scientists brewed worms with a genetically modified nervous system that can be controlled using hypulses of sound waves.
Tiny nematodes change the direction of motion at the moment when they are influenced by sound pulses too high that the human ear can hear them.
Impulses have an effect by activating neurons genetically modified to use membrane channels that respond to ultrasound waves.
Explarators Manipulate Mottivary Activity With Light
Scientists said that worms demonstrate the capabilities of a new technique called "sonogenetic", the essence of which is to use ultrasound to excrete certain cells of the brain, heart and muscle, while the source of ultrasound is located outside the body.
Śrīnant Chalasani, a researcher at the Salque Institute, La Hoya, California, told the Guardian that this technique could once replace deep brain stimulus - an invasive procedure that sends electrical impulses to a person's brain and which is used to treat Parkinson's disease.
Nematode worms don’t usually respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani found that they started responding when they were placed in a fluid containing microscopic vesic bubbles.
He found that bubbles amplify ultrasonic waves, which then enveloped into the worms.
Amplified ultrasonic waves affect structures referred to “TRP-4 ion canals” found in the membranes of certain worm cells.
Ultra-sonic waves cause these ion channels to open and excrete the cells that are associated with them, according to a report in Nature Communications.
To create nematodes, which can be controlled by ultrasound, Chalasani made genetic modification of worms, so that their propulsion neurons used TRP-4 ion channels.
During the use of ultrasound to influencing modified organisms, sound waves were amplified with microscopic vesic buffs and worm bodies, activating modified motor neurons.
This technique is similar to the one that is used in "optogenetics" - a new direction, which allows scientists to include and turn off neurons with the help of light pulses.
Chalasani argues that compared to this technique, soneticirology can have some benefits.
In contrast to light, which is delivered to the desired part of the brain on fiber, low-frequency ultrasonic waves freely pass through the tissues of the body, so you do not need to pass inside the skull.
“We believe that for the treatment of certain neurological diseases in a clinical setting, the use of gene therapies and therapeutic viruses can temporarily make human neurons sensitive to an ultrasound signal,” Chalasani said.
He added that, in addition, the technique can be applied to musculoskeletal and insulin-producing cells.
