Physicians are urging to ban boxing.
The management of the Australian medical Association (AMA) called to ban boxing in the country after the death of local athlete David Brown Jr.
On Monday, the 28-year-old Brown died from injuries received in a duel with Filipino Carlo Magali.
The battle for one of the regional titles according to the International Boxing Federation (IBF) in the weight category up to 57.15 kog, held on Friday, culminated in the victory of Magala by knockout in the 12th round.
Brown was taken to the hospital with a cerebjuvenation.
Its condition was evaluated as critical, and it was connected to a life support device.
In the professional ring, Brown won 22 victories, suffered only two defeats and one fight ended in a draw.
He's got two children.
This is the second case of death among Australian boxers in the last six months.
23-Year-Old student Braydon Smith died from injuries sustained in the ring.
Now AMA recommends not to engage in this type of sport, but better, from its point of view, to forbid it at all.
“I understand that this happens unintentionally, – said Vice-president Steven Parrnis.
But in the form in the form in which there is now a box, there will always be situations when the athlete receives hemorrhage or brain injury, which leads either to death or to severe damage.
For this reason, AMA believes that we can't continue to live with it.
The Australian Medical Association - an independent professional structure, which unites practicing doctors and medical students.
Sergei Naryshkin called on the PA of the ACE to lift sanctions against parliamentarians of the Russian Federation.
