“We went on dubious trains and buses, was hungry, thirsty, cold and fascinated,” she wrote.
I'm lucky.
But since the refugee and migrant crisis in Europe has increased, a lot of people such as my family are desperately in need of help.
Donations will be donated to four non-commercial organizations advocating for refugees and migrants: “Doctors Without Borders”, International Rescue Committee, Save the Children and the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refuge.
Google said it would reprise $15.5 million until google.com/refugeerieLeief reached $11 million.
“Europe is confronting the strongest refugee crisis since World War II, triggered mostly by a four-Year-Lithuanian Civil War in Syria that has moved more than $4 million this year.
Many are also fleeing war-strapped Afghanistan, Yemen, Iraq and Libya.
Report - Yasmin Abutaleb; editor - Christian Plum
Diabetics with minor Obesity May Help Obesity Surgery
Favorable after obescence surgery in people with minor-obete suffering from type 2 diabetes can last at least five years, according to a new study.
However, now it is too early to claim that diabetics with minor obesity live longer after obesan surgery than those who receive non-surgical treatment.
“The mortality statistics don’t appear at once at all,” said Dr. Robin Blackstone, an expert on obessia surgery, who wrote about a new study in JAMA Surgery’s columb.
Obesity Surgery or Bariatric Surgery uses various methods to reduce the size of the gastric gauge.
They reduce the feeling of starvation and limit the ability of organizations to assimilate food.
For several years, bariatric Surgery has proved its efficacy to treat type 2 diabetic, but basic studies were carried out among people with morbid obespite body mass (BMI) 35 or higher.
A BMI, the measure of weight relative to the increase, is considered normal with a value of 18,5 to 24.
A BMI of 25 or higher indicates that a person is overweight, and people with a BITE are considered to be obsolete.
You can calculate your BMI here: 1.usa.gov/1DZqDv.
For a new study, researchers from the Taynian Min-Sheng General hospital used data collected since 2007, to compare two types of bariatric Surgery - gastric bypass and sleeving gastrectomy, with a medical treatment of type 2 diab disease in people with minor obession.
An average BMI in those who have undergodried surgery has fallen from 31 to 24.5 by the end of the fifth year of their involvement in the study.
At the same time, BMI in those receiving non-surgical treatment for diabetes, it stays at about level.
Among those who underwent the operation were completely cured of diabetic 36%, and in part - 28%.
In the group that received drug treatment, for comparison, a complete cure for diabetes was observed only in 1%, and partially - only in about 2%.
In addition, blood pressure, triglycerides and "bad" LDL cholesterol "generally were better in the surgical group," the authors said.
The researchers also monitored blood hemogglobin levels that reflected blood sugar levels in dynamics.
Hemoglobin A1c is better to keep below 7%, - the researchers write.
After surgical intervention, the average hemoglobine A1c level decreased from about 9% to about 6%.
In the therapeutic group, however, it remained steadfaytious at about 8%.
But the best glycemic controls, reflected by good hemoglobine A1c hemoglobine levels, did not reduce the mortality rate over the five-year years, according to the head of the study, Dr. Chi-Cheng-Su, and his colleagues.
The research team also compared the average results of the two types of surgical treatment.
For the fifth year, compared to the group that received the sleeve gastrechtomy, the bypass group lost more weight (18.7 kilo kg against 14.2 kilograms), achieved a greater decrease in a BMI (7.4 against 5.1) and hemoglobine A1s (3.1% against 2.1%), and was also closer to a complete remittance of diabetes (46.9% against 16.7%).
Although the new study does not demonstrate the advantages of the surgical method in terms of life expectancy after a five-Year life expectancy, there is evidence based on the results of a study of overweight people in Sweden that surgical treatment actually leads to an increase in life expectancy, according to Blackston from the College of Medicine in Phoenix, University of Arizona.
According to a Swedish study for obescians who chose bariatric intermittance, the probability of dying for 15 years was about 29% lower compared to a group that used more traditional methods.
Even though a new study didn’t show an increase in life expectancy, Blackstone noted that it encourages people with an IMT below 35 interested in such a procedure.
“Here the report is very important, because it argues that such surgical intervention is safe in the group with a low BMI where there is an increased risk of mortality or renal diseases,” she said.
Backstone added that people and their doctors should begin to take weight seriously, as soon as the patient's BMI falls into the range between 27 and 30.
“I think we’ve waited too long to force people to take it seriously,” she said.
It seems to me that as soon as they accumulate the genetic changes that start obessia and diabetes in their bodies, it is very difficult to reverse.
In Mytishchi, the robbers were taken out of the house fur coarse and 9 million rubble.
In Mytishchi plumbers attacked the residents of the suburban house and stole 9 million rubles and three women's fur coats.
About it informs "Interfax" with reference to the press service of the Ministry of Internal Affairs of the Russian Federation.
The incident took place on Tuesday in the morning.
The two unidentified persons through an open front door entered the house, located on Gorky street, tied the 20-year-old son of the mistress of the house and, threatening with both knive, stole 9 million rubles and three fur coats.
