Hewlett-Packard will reduce up to 30 000 jobs
Hewlett-Packard intends to reduce from 25 000 to 30 000 jobs as part of its restructuring and reduced costs in the field of services.
In the same year, Hewlett-Packard will be divided into two listed companies, separating the production of computers and printers from the faster-growing sectors of hardware and service operations.
Debt as a result of the estimated job cuts will amount to about 2.7 billion US dollars since the fourth-to-creation, Hewlett-Packard said in a statement.
“Such a restructuring will provide a more competitive, sustainable spending structure for the new Hewlett Packard enterprise,” said Meg Whitman, chairman and CEO of Hewlett Packard, which will lead the organization after separation.
Hewlett Packard Enterprise will be smaller and more concentrated than Hewlett-Packard at the present time, and the portfolio of business directions will be wider and deeper, which will help the company move to a new business styling,” Whitman said in a statement.
As a separate company, we are in a better position than before to respond to the growing requests of our customers around the world.
Hewlett Packard Enterprise will have an annual revenue of more than U.S. $50 billion and "will focus on providing previously unattainable integrated technology solutions," the company said in a statement.
This divides the company, founded in the 1930-ies by Stanford graduators Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard to releasing electrical equipment; their garage in Palo Alto was nicknamed Silicon Valley “the birthplace”.
Hewlett Packard is in the process of large-scale reorganization in order to transition from traditional personal computers to mobile devices.
Such a step HP, the world’s second maker of personal computers and one of U.S.’s largest tech companies, is the newest event in this sector, based on the belief that highly specialized companies are working better.
